WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.937
In the lungs, inhaled air reaches tiny alveoli
beside pulmonary capillaries.

2
00:00:05.937 --> 00:00:10.586
Oxygen crosses the thin respiratory membrane
because its partial pressure is higher in

3
00:00:10.586 --> 00:00:13.090
alveolar air than in the arriving blood.

4
00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:18.953
Diffusion continues toward equilibrium; the
membrane does not actively pump oxygen into

5
00:00:18.953 --> 00:00:19.930
the bloodstream.

6
00:00:20.042 --> 00:00:23.510
Only a small amount of oxygen remains
dissolved in plasma.

7
00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:27.596
Most enters red blood cells and binds
reversibly to hemoglobin.

8
00:00:27.596 --> 00:00:32.711
This transport system greatly increases how
much oxygen the blood can carry.

9
00:00:32.711 --> 00:00:37.862
Oxygenated blood then returns to the heart and
is pumped through the systemic circulation.

10
00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:43.314
Active tissues consume oxygen during cellular
respiration, lowering oxygen partial pressure

11
00:00:43.314 --> 00:00:44.771
around their cells.

12
00:00:44.771 --> 00:00:50.758
That gradient favors oxygen leaving the blood,
separating from hemoglobin, and diffusing into

13
00:00:50.758 --> 00:00:51.218
tissue.

14
00:00:51.218 --> 00:00:57.234
The complete path is driven by linked
gradients: ventilation refreshes alveolar air,

15
00:00:57.234 --> 00:01:00.744
circulation moves blood, and metabolism
maintains demand.
